    "description": "An insurance policy system stores rich domain objects (policies, coverages, endorsements) in a legacy schema with 30 tables whose column names match neither the class attributes nor the current model version. The persistence code is written per service: each one assembles SQL and pulls column values out of the ResultSet in its own order, so a schema change breaks three services and one loader silently swapped two money columns. The mapping must survive schema changes and model refactors independently, keep the domain classes free of persistence annotations, and make it possible to add a second store (document storage for the archive) later. The decision to implement: how to translate between in-memory domain objects and the relational tables so both sides can evolve independently.",
    "decision_statement": "Translate between domain objects and relational tables in one layer so model and schema evolve independently.",
    "notes": "Holdout family: active-record is the acceptable near miss when persistence and domain coincide."
  },
